<!--
  move_3d.xml: move.xml for a 3D map (feature/octomap-planner-3d, 2026-10-19).
  The task_runner picks it instead of move.xml, per goal, while the planner's
  Octo3D has a map (it says so on the latched planner_server/Octo3D/
  map_available): a goal with no behavior_tree, or one naming move.xml, runs
  this file on a 3D map and move.xml -- dev's 2D tree, unchanged -- on a 2D
  one. A map is one or the other, never both (2026-10-19), so the planner's
  state is the map's type. Until then this file WAS move.xml on this branch,
  and every goal planned on the octree, so a 2D map failed them all. The
  section "3D navigation" below is what differs from move.xml; the rest of
  this header is move.xml's, kept with the tree it describes.

  Navigate-to-pose, replanning only when the current path becomes invalid, with
  contextual recovery. Based on nav2's
  navigate_w_replanning_only_if_path_becomes_invalid.xml but without its outer
  system-level RecoveryNode: a planner / controller failure clears its own
  costmap and retries once, since stale obstacles in the costmap are the most
  common cause. nav2's system-level branch (clear both costmaps,
  Spin/Wait/BackUp via RoundRobin, abort on GoalUpdated) is left out for now;
  most of its BT nodes and the behavior server are not ported yet. The case
  BackUp is for, a robot boxed in so that every MPPI candidate collides, is
  the controller's own since 2026-10-25: FollowPath backs out up to
  back_out_dist once per path (controller_server_params.yaml), so each
  FollowPath retry below starts from where that left the robot.

  Why not replan every tick (this tree did, at 1 Hz, until 2026-10): a fresh
  plan always wins, so the route is only as stable as the costmap's memory of
  what it has seen. The global costmap's obstacle layer raytraces the 3D cloud
  in 2D, so a ray to a wall point above a low blocker clears the blocker's cell
  as soon as the robot can see past it. Half-way round a detour the original
  route then read free and shortest again, and the robot turned back into the
  blocker. Now a path is kept until IsPathValid sees, on the part still ahead,
  a centre cell at >= INSCRIBED cost (the planner's own rejection test) or a
  LETHAL cell under the padded footprint's perimeter at the path heading
  +-0.35 rad (the test RPP applies before it drives, run on the global costmap
  while the blocker is still 2.5 m out -- without it a blocker 0.25-0.45 m
  beside the route passed the centre test, RPP refused the path, and the goal
  aborted with no detour), or the goal changes. The price: a blocker that
  leaves does not shorten the route back; the detour is finished. That is the
  recoverable side, and no "switch when the new path is X% shorter" threshold
  had to be invented to get it.

  Every new goal starts by clearing the global costmap (2026-10-14). The
  obstacle layer only clears a mark by raytracing through it, out to
  raytrace_max_range (3 m), so a mark the robot drives away from is kept for
  good, and the next goal planned round it. On robot01 the return leg of an
  A <-> B shuttle came out 60 m instead of 17.5 m: 509 LETHAL cells, absent
  from the static map, closed the corridor the previous leg had just driven
  (most likely people beside or behind the robot, whose bodies hid their own
  trail from the rays). Re-solving that costmap offline gave the same 60 m,
  so the planner was right and the memory was stale. The clear is cheap and
  safe: the planner waits for the costmap to be current again (<= 1 s at
  1 Hz) before it plans; that update re-copies the static map and marks the
  latest cloud, so a blocker within the 2.5 m obstacle range is back before
  the first plan; one further out is marked on approach and IsPathValid
  replans round it, the same as a blocker that appears mid-drive; and the
  keepout mask survives (the filter re-subscribes, logging "New filter mask
  arrived", but swaps masks under its lock and never runs without one). Only
  at tree start, so a preempt (the tree keeps running) does not clear: the
  memory that makes a detour stick is the point mid-goal. ForceSuccess,
  because a clear that times out (1 s; it waits on the costmap lock, which
  an update can hold well past the 20 ms default) must not fail the goal --
  the stale-memory route is still a route. Side effect: ClearEntireCostmap
  counts itself into number_recoveries, so the feedback's
  number_of_recoveries now starts at 1 on every goal (nothing reads it today).

  The 1 Hz RateController now paces validity checks; a plan only runs when the
  check fails, so a blocked path is still noticed within ~1 s of the global
  costmap (also 1 Hz) marking it. IsPathValid gets 100 ms instead of the 20 ms
  default_server_timeout: it answers after taking the costmap lock, which a
  costmap update can hold for longer than 20 ms. A timeout keeps the path (a
  replan nobody can justify is the thing this tree exists to avoid; the next
  check is a second away and the controller holds the robot meanwhile). A timeout WARN in
  the task_runner log on every tick means the costmap update and the check
  have phase-locked: raise server_timeout here.

  A blocked path is replanned only once it has stayed blocked for 10 s
  (blocked_time, 2026-10-14): the same spot of the same path (within 0.5 m,
  blocked_match_distance) answered blocked by every check for that long,
  with no clear answer in between. Until then the path is kept, the
  task_runner log says "blocked at (x, y) for n of 10 s; keeping it" and the
  status sentence says the robot reroutes if it is still blocked after 10 s;
  one clear answer ends the wait without a replan. Before that day one answer
  was a replan, and the global costmap marks anything crossing the route within
  2.5 m, so a person walking across bought a detour that stuck for the rest
  of the goal (see above): on robot01 (2026-10-14) a ~11 m leg flipped to a
  ~35 m detour and back, one blocked answer per switch. Three checks in a
  row (~2 s) was tried first that day; 10 s also waits out a person who
  stops in the way, which is when a detour is least wanted. Meanwhile MPPI
  bends round the blocker or stands in front of it. "0" is the old
  behaviour. The wait restarts on any new path, so it also spaces out a
  replan whose own result reads blocked. blocked_time is bounded by
  FollowPath's retries below: lengthen one, re-check the other.

  FollowPath gets four retries, and they refill (retry_refill_time, 2026-10).
  The count is how long the robot waits for a blocker that stands in front of
  it. FollowPath is MPPI since 2026-10 (controller_server_params.yaml), which
  bends round a blocker beside the path inside the local costmap; one that
  closes the way leaves it no feasible trajectory ("Optimizer fail to compute
  path"), and there is no Wait recovery, so from then on the wait is
  failure_tolerance (3 s) times retries + 1, with a local costmap clear
  between attempts (re-marked in 0.2 s; both controllers zero their accel
  baseline on every refusal, so no lurch). Until then RPP was FollowPath, a
  tracker that refused at any blocker in its projection; it is still loaded
  as FollowPathRPP, and controller_id="FollowPathRPP" below is the whole A/B. One
  retry (~6 s) was enough for a person walking across and not for one
  standing and talking; three (~12 s) covered that, at the price of a task
  failure taking ~12 s instead of ~6 s to declare. Four (~16 s, 2026-10-14)
  because the wait now has to outlast IsPathValid's blocked_time (10 s): a
  person who steps in close makes MPPI refuse at once, while the 1 Hz global
  costmap and the 1 Hz check can take ~2 s more to report the path blocked,
  so the replan lands ~13 s into the refusals, and three retries run out at
  ~12-13 s, just before it (estimated, not measured). Keep (retries + 1) x 3 s at
  least blocked_time + 4 s (15 s would need six). The progress checker's
  30 s movement_time_allowance remains the outer bound. The refill: the
  RecoveryNode only resets when it returns, and FollowPath is RUNNING for the
  whole goal, so the retries used to be per goal -- a blocker met early used
  them up and the next one, however much later, failed the goal outright. An
  attempt that ran at least 60 s before failing now earns them back. 60 s is
  twice movement_time_allowance: an attempt that never gets going fails
  sooner than that (3 s of failure_tolerance, or ~30 s without progress), so
  a dead end still runs out of retries; it must not drop to 30 or below. The
  planner branch's RecoveryNode keeps one retry and needs no refill:
  ComputePathToPose returns each tick, which resets that node.

  Failure attribution (2026-10). The navigator fills NavigateToGoal's
  error_code from the blackboard keys failed_node / failure_msg, which
  ComputePathToPose and FollowPath write when their goal is aborted (last
  writer wins; blackboard_keys.hpp). The tree's shape is what makes that
  correct, so it is load-bearing: PipelineSequence ticks the planner branch
  (child 0) before FollowPath (child 1) and returns at the first FAILURE. If
  the planner branch fails the tree, child 1 is not ticked in that tick, so
  ComputePathToPose's second abort is the last write. If FollowPath fails the
  tree in a tick where ComputePathToPose also aborted (its retry pending
  inside a RecoveryNode that returns RUNNING), child 1 is ticked after child
  0, so FollowPath wrote last. Either way the key names the branch that
  actually failed the tree, and a node that aborted and was retried
  successfully is overwritten by whichever node fails later. The
  ClearEntireCostmap nodes stay silent on purpose: a recovery one only runs
  because an action node just failed, so the last action-node write already
  names the right branch, and a service timeout there would otherwise
  overwrite it with something that maps to UNKNOWN. (The new-goal clear runs
  before either branch and, under ForceSuccess, never fails the tree, so it
  has nothing to name either.) Accepted limitation: a successfully retried
  abort leaves its entry behind, so a tree that later fails through a path
  that writes nothing (an exception) reports the stale one.

  Status sentence (2026-10). NavigateToGoal's feedback status_msg is the
  blackboard key nav_status_msg, written at each turn of this tree so the
  console can say more than "running": IsPathValid ("Path blocked;
  replanning") and GlobalUpdatedGoal ("Goal changed; replanning") when the
  planner branch is about to replan, ComputePathToPose when a path lands
  ("Following the path") or the planner gives up, FollowPath when the
  controller gives up and when its retry goes out. Same last-writer-wins
  channel as above, and the same child order makes it read right: a replan
  started by child 0 is written before FollowPath is ticked, and FollowPath
  only overwrites its own retry sentence, so "replanning" stands while the
  robot keeps driving the old path, until the new one lands.

  3D navigation (2026-10-14, feature/octomap-planner-3d). This tree asks for
  planner_id="Octo3D" (OctomapPlanner3D on octomap.bt, the only planner that
  routes between floors over a stair link), not GridBased. The controller is
  move.xml's FollowPath (MPPI) since 2026-10-20: it matches a path with
  heights in 3D and climbs stairs with the same state machine
  (syncai_util::StairMode) that RPP 3D introduced, with the optimizer skipped
  on the flight and running everywhere else, so the local detours MPPI gives
  the 2D tree are the 3D tree's too -- on the local costmap's terrain layer,
  which runs on a 3D map in place of the obstacle layer and judges steps,
  slopes, drops, stairs and low obstacles against the ground under the
  robot. A flight itself is climbed blind: no obstacle avoidance on stairs,
  by decision (2026-10-20). From 2026-10-19 until then this line named
  FollowPathRPP3D (syncai_controller's RegulatedPurePursuitController3D),
  the first controller to track heights and switch the gait policy, at the
  price of every detour: it is still loaded, and controller_id="FollowPathRPP3D"
  here is the A/B on a 3D map, as FollowPathRPP is on a 2D one. move.xml is
  the 2D stack; nobody edits ids to switch any more.

  IsPathValid is in this tree again since 2026-10-21, and everything the
  paragraphs above say about it, blocked_time and the FollowPath retries
  holds here. What changed to make that true: Octo3D keeps a dynamic
  overlay of its own (syncai_global_planner, dynamic_overlay.hpp) -- the
  same lidar cloud the costmaps read, in the map frame, every return
  0.1-0.6 m above the surface it lies on a mark on that cell (the surface
  is picked by height, so two floors never see each other's people), rays
  clearing the marks they cross, marks forgotten after 30 s -- and
  planner_server's is_path_valid asks the planner that made the last plan
  when that planner does not use the costmap, so an Octo3D path is judged
  on that overlay and never on the 2D costmap. (That costmap was why the
  check was taken out on 2026-10-19: judged on the ground floor's gridmap,
  a path upstairs read blocked every second, and a replan on the static
  octree could only return the same route.) A replan detours now: the
  search keeps off the marks and 0.35 m round them (the robot's half-width
  plus the margin the 2D footprint test sweeps) and pays for the band
  beyond as it does for a wall, so the route bends round a person with the
  room it has, and NavigateToGoal's number_of_replans is meaningful on a 3D
  map. Two rules of the check are the planner's and not the 2D test's: it
  judges only the floor stretch the robot is on and answers "valid" while
  the robot is on a stair, because a replan from mid-flight would snap its
  start to the wrong floor and a goal aborting on a flight stops the robot
  there, so a person at the stair head is judged once the robot is on that
  floor; and the 0.35 m round the robot's own cell is exempt from the
  marks' dilation (as the planner exempts the start), so a person beside
  the robot does not block the pose under it with nothing a replan could
  change. A person inside the 0.25 m wall ring is not marked at all; the
  local costmap's terrain layer and MPPI deal with them.

  Both ClearEntireCostmap nodes call planner_server/Octo3D/clear_overlay
  (nav2_msgs/srv/ClearEntireCostmap, the costmap clear's own type, so the
  node is move.xml's) instead of the global costmap's clear, which is a
  no-op for Octo3D. The semantics are move.xml's: every new goal starts
  from a clean memory -- a mark the robot walked away from is not
  raytraced away here either, and though it ages out after 30 s a goal sent
  sooner must not plan round a person who has left -- and a plan that fails
  clears and retries once. One difference: after its clear move.xml's
  planner waits for the costmap to be current, which re-marks a blocker
  within 2.5 m before the first plan; Octo3D does not wait, so the first
  plan after a clear can run through a person in view until the next scan
  (<= 0.1 s) marks them and IsPathValid replans after blocked_time.
  Accepted: the stale-memory route is the worse failure.

  From 2026-10-19 to 2026-10-21 this tree planned once per goal
  (number_plans == 0 in place of IsPathValid), so nothing replanned on a 3D
  map and the FollowPath retries were the only wait for a blocker that
  closes the way -- ~16 s on paper, in practice the progress checker's
  30 s per attempt, since MPPI stands still legally in front of a person who
  stands still and never trips failure_tolerance.

  Costmap clear services live on the costmap nodes owned by the planner
  (global_costmap) and controller (local_costmap). Relative service names
  resolve under the robot namespace because every node in the stack shares it.
-->
<root main_tree_to_execute="MainTree">
  <BehaviorTree ID="MainTree">
    <Sequence name="NavigateFromFreshGlobalCostmap">
      <ForceSuccess>
        <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearDynamicOverlay-NewGoal" service_name="planner_server/Octo3D/clear_overlay" server_timeout="1000"/>
      </ForceSuccess>
      <PipelineSequence name="NavigateWithReplanning">
        <RateController hz="1.0">
          <RecoveryNode number_of_retries="1" name="ComputePathToPose">
            <Fallback name="FallbackComputePathToPose">
              <ReactiveSequence name="CheckIfNewPathNeeded">
                <Inverter>
                  <GlobalUpdatedGoal/>
                </Inverter>
                <!-- Judged by Octo3D on its dynamic overlay (the planner server
                     asks the planner that made the last plan); see the header. -->
                <IsPathValid path="{path}" server_timeout="100" blocked_time="10.0"/>
              </ReactiveSequence>
              <ComputePathToPose goal="{goal}" path="{path}" planner_id="Octo3D"/>
            </Fallback>
            <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearDynamicOverlay-Context" service_name="planner_server/Octo3D/clear_overlay"/>
          </RecoveryNode>
        </RateController>
        <RecoveryNode number_of_retries="4" retry_refill_time="60.0" name="FollowPath">
          <FollowPath path="{path}" controller_id="FollowPath"/>
          <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearLocalCostmap-Context" service_name="local_costmap/clear_entirely_local_costmap"/>
        </RecoveryNode>
      </PipelineSequence>
    </Sequence>
  </BehaviorTree>
</root>
